UPDATE public.crossings SET
  hazards = '["Blue sharks (Prionace glauca) and occasional shortfin mako sharks are common in the Santa Catalina Basin — shark watches are mandatory.","Portuguese man-o-war and moon jellies can appear in concentrated blooms, causing stings across face and shoulders.","Cold thermocline upwelling mid-channel can drop water temp by 4–6°C within metres, triggering cold shock.","Heavy commercial and recreational vessel traffic in the San Pedro Channel requires escort boats to maintain safety perimeter."]'::jsonb,
  conditions = '["Prevailing northwest swells (1–2m) create an uncomfortable side chop for most of the crossing.","Fog is common in June and July, reducing visibility to near zero — GPS navigation and horn signals become critical.","Wind picks up in the afternoon, so most swimmers aim to finish before 2pm; a midnight start is standard.","Kelp forests near both coastlines can entangle swimmers and must be navigated carefully at the start and finish."]'::jsonb,
  about_md = 'The Catalina Channel separates Santa Catalina Island from the California mainland across the San Pedro Channel. The first recorded crossing was completed by Canadian swimmer George Young in 1927, who won the Wrigley Ocean Marathon, a race organised by chewing gum magnate William Wrigley Jr. with a $25,000 prize. Young completed the swim in 15 hours 44 minutes, beating 102 other contestants in what was one of the most watched sporting events of the era.

The standard sanctioned route runs from the Isthmus Cove area at Two Harbors on Catalina''s north coast to the mainland finish at Point Vicente Lighthouse on the Palos Verdes Peninsula. Swimmers may also complete the reverse (mainland to island) or "double Catalina" crossing — both count toward the Triple Crown. Diana Nyad completed the crossing in 1977 and holds a notable place in the channel''s history.

The Catalina Channel Swimming Federation, founded in 1981, sets the rules for sanctioned swims. English Channel rules apply: no wetsuit, no touching the boat, and solo or relay attempts must be ratified by an official observer. Relay teams of up to 6 swimmers are common, with each swimmer taking 1-hour rotations.

The crossing is part of the Triple Crown of Open Water Swimming alongside the English Channel and the Manhattan Island Marathon Swim — a recognition given to swimmers who complete all three. As of 2024, fewer than 250 individuals have achieved the Triple Crown.',
  preparation = '["Longest single open water swim of at least 20–25km before your attempt.","Weekly training volume of 40–50km in the 3 months prior to the crossing.","Sustained swimming in water below 16°C — cold acclimation is non-negotiable.","Night swimming experience: most crossings start in darkness and last until late morning.","Feed practice from a moving boat — you will need to eat and drink every 30–45 minutes without stopping.","Most coaches recommend a 12–18 month preparation window for first-time Catalina Channel aspirants. The key physical demands are different from pool marathon swimming: you need cold acclimatisation more than raw speed, and the ability to maintain your pace when jellies sting and the thermocline kicks in at hour six. Build a base of long open water swims through winter to train your body to stay efficient in 14–16°C water.","Nutrition is a critical discipline. Over a 12–14 hour swim you will consume 4,000–6,000 calories, primarily from liquid feeds (maltodextrin-based drinks, warm broth, gels). Practice your feed protocol extensively in training so it becomes automatic. Your escort crew — captain, observer, and at least two support people — need to be briefed on the CCSF rules and your feeding schedule. Engage a local Catalina crew well in advance; experienced captains book out a year ahead."]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"q":"Do I need to swim from island to mainland, or can I go the other direction?","a":"Both directions are accepted by CCSF for ratification. Most swimmers go island to mainland (Two Harbors to Pt Vicente) as prevailing currents are slightly more favourable, but the reverse route is equally valid for the Triple Crown."},{"q":"Are wetsuits allowed?","a":"No. CCSF rules mirror English Channel rules — only a standard swimsuit, cap, and goggles are permitted for a ratified solo crossing. Relay swimmers rotate under the same rules. Wetsuits disqualify a ratified attempt."},{"q":"How do I apply for a sanctioned crossing?","a":"Apply through the CCSF website (swimcatalina.org). You submit a swim history demonstrating readiness, pay the sanctioning fee, and arrange your own escort boat from the CCSF-approved captain list. Observer assignment comes from CCSF. Slots fill months in advance."},{"q":"What happens if there are sharks?","a":"Blue sharks are common and generally not aggressive toward swimmers. Your escort boat crew maintains a watch and will advise you if they feel a shark poses a threat. The swimmer makes the final decision whether to continue. There are no recorded shark attacks on Catalina Channel swimmers, but the risk is real and must be accepted before you start."},{"q":"What is the typical cost of a Catalina Channel crossing?","a":"Budget USD $3,000–$5,000 for the attempt including escort boat charter (the largest expense at $2,000–$3,500), CCSF sanctioning fees, observer fees, travel, and accommodation on Catalina the night before. Relay teams split the boat cost between swimmers."}]'::jsonb,
  duration_text = '12–14 hrs',
  governing_body = 'CCSF',
  season_start_month = 6,
  season_end_month = 10,
  start_location = 'Two Harbors, Catalina Island',
  end_location = 'Pt Vicente Lighthouse, Palos Verdes Peninsula',
  oceans_seven = NULL,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE slug = 'catalina-channel';

UPDATE public.crossings SET
  hazards = '["Water temperature of 10–15°C is cold enough to cause progressive hypothermia over a long swim — cold acclimatisation is more critical here than almost any other Oceans Seven crossing.","Tidal currents in Cook Strait can reach 4 knots and change direction abruptly. A swimmer caught in the wrong current can be pushed miles off course in minutes.","Weather instability: Cook Strait funnels the Roaring Forties — westerly winds that circle the Southern Ocean. Storms can arrive with very little warning, making conditions dangerous within an hour.","Great white sharks are present in Cook Strait and the surrounding waters. Shark cage escorts are not used — swimmers proceed in open water with their escort boat as the only protection."]'::jsonb,
  conditions = '["The strait acts as a wind and current amplifier between the Tasman Sea and the Pacific Ocean — conditions are almost never flat calm.","Swell from the Tasman Sea and Southern Ocean creates confused, cross-directional seas that make stroke rhythm difficult to maintain for hours.","Visibility can drop to near zero in squalls; pilot vessels must maintain close contact throughout.","The strong tidal flow means swims rarely travel in a straight line — expert pilot navigation is the difference between a successful crossing and a failed one."]'::jsonb,
  about_md = 'Cook Strait is the body of water separating New Zealand''s two main islands, connecting the Tasman Sea to the northwest with the South Pacific Ocean to the southeast. Named after British explorer James Cook, who first charted it in 1770, the strait is one of the world''s most heavily trafficked ferry crossings — the Interislander and Bluebridge ferries make the crossing multiple times daily between Wellington and Picton. Sharing this water with commercial shipping adds an additional navigation challenge for swimmers.

The first recorded swim of Cook Strait was by Barry Devonport in 1962, completing the crossing in 11 hours 20 minutes from the South Island to the North. Since then, fewer than 80 people have completed the crossing — a testament to its difficulty. Philip Rush''s 1988 record of 7 hours 20 minutes remains one of the most remarkable feats in open water swimming history, particularly given the water temperature and conditions he would have faced.

Cook Strait is one of the seven crossings that make up the Oceans Seven — the open water swimming equivalent of the Seven Summits mountaineering challenge. The other six are the English Channel, the Catalina Channel, the Molokai Channel, the Tsugaru Strait, the Strait of Gibraltar, and the North Channel (Ireland to Scotland). Cook Strait is widely regarded as one of the two or three most difficult of the seven, alongside the North Channel and the Molokai Channel.

The Cook Strait Swimming Association, formed to support and ratify crossings, works with a small number of experienced local pilots who understand the strait''s currents intimately. Their knowledge is irreplaceable — the tidal patterns in Cook Strait are complex, highly localised, and cannot be reliably planned from tables alone. Engaging an CSSA-approved pilot is non-negotiable for a serious attempt.',
  preparation = '["Cold water acclimatisation to sustained swimming in 10–13°C — this is the defining preparation challenge for Cook Strait.","Longest single open water swim of at least 20km before your attempt.","Weekly volume of 40–50km in the 3 months prior, with regular cold water sessions through winter.","Experience in rough, confused seas — swell training in exposed ocean conditions, not just harbour swims.","Psychological preparation for extended weather waits — many aspiring Cook Strait swimmers travel to Wellington and wait 1–2 weeks for a suitable window.","Cold water acclimatisation is the most important and most underestimated aspect of Cook Strait preparation. At 10–12°C, the water is cold enough to cause significant pain to unacclimated swimmers within the first hour, and progressive hypothermia over the 8–14 hours a crossing typically takes. You must train your body to function efficiently — maintaining pace, breathing rhythm, and mental clarity — in water this cold. This takes months of year-round cold water exposure, not a few preparatory dips before you fly to Wellington.","Plan for a flexible travel schedule. The weather window in Cook Strait is notoriously narrow and unpredictable. Most experienced Oceans Seven chasers allocate 2–3 weeks in New Zealand and treat each day as a potential go day. This requires flexibility from your employer, family, and support crew. The CSSA and your pilot will monitor conditions daily and call the start typically 12–24 hours in advance. Some swimmers have made multiple trips to Wellington before conditions finally aligned for a successful attempt."]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"q":"Which direction is the swim — South Island to North, or North to South?","a":"Both directions are ratified by the CSSA. The majority of successful crossings have been South Island to North Island (ending near Wellington), but the reverse has also been completed. Direction is chosen based on prevailing weather and current patterns at the time of the attempt — your pilot will advise on the best option for your window."},{"q":"How do I contact the Cook Strait Swimming Association?","a":"The CSSA is a small volunteer organisation. Initial contact is best made via their published contact details and well in advance of your planned attempt. They will connect you with approved pilots and advise on the sanctioning process. Build in at least 12 months of lead time for planning purposes."},{"q":"What is the success rate for Cook Strait attempts?","a":"The success rate is difficult to precisely quantify because many attempts are aborted due to weather before the swimmer even enters the water. Of swims that begin, a significant proportion are pulled due to weather deterioration mid-crossing. Successful completions represent a fraction of total attempts — this is primarily a weather statistic rather than a fitness one."},{"q":"Are there sharks in Cook Strait?","a":"Yes. Great white sharks inhabit these waters, attracted by the large seal and sea lion populations around the Cook Strait coastlines. No swimmer has been attacked during a ratified Cook Strait attempt, but the risk is acknowledged and accepted by all who attempt the crossing. Shark cage swimming is not an option for a ratified Oceans Seven attempt."},{"q":"How cold is 10–15°C in practice?","a":"At 10°C, most unacclimated swimmers would become incapacitated within 1–3 hours. At 13–15°C, a well-acclimated swimmer can sustain effort for many hours but will experience progressive core temperature drop. Cook Strait swimmers typically finish with core temperatures of 35–36°C — clinically mild hypothermia. Immediate warming protocol post-swim is essential. This is not water you improvise in without extensive cold acclimatisation."}]'::jsonb,
  duration_text = '8–14 hrs',
  governing_body = 'CSSA',
  season_start_month = 1,
  season_end_month = 3,
  start_location = 'South Island (Turakirae Head area)',
  end_location = 'North Island (Oteranga Bay, Wellington)',
  oceans_seven = true,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE slug = 'cook-strait';

UPDATE public.crossings SET
  hazards = '["Great white sharks — False Bay is a known great white aggregation site, particularly around Seal Island","The south-east wind (''the Cape Doctor'') builds aggressively through the day, creating short steep chop","Distance across open water with no intermediate land — a 10+ hour commitment","Cold water intrusion from the Atlantic side can drop temperatures unexpectedly mid-crossing"]'::jsonb,
  conditions = '["Start windows are early morning (05:00–06:00) before the south-easter activates","Swell from the south-west enters the mouth of the bay and can create confused sea states","Water temperature varies across the bay — the Gordon''s Bay end is warmer than the Simon''s Town end","Visibility under water is generally good in settled conditions"]'::jsonb,
  about_md = 'False Bay is a large bay on the south-western tip of South Africa, flanked by the Cape Peninsula on the west and the Hottentots Holland mountains on the east. The bay faces south-east, directly into the Southern Ocean, which drives the dominant swell and wind pattern.

A crossing of False Bay — typically from Miller''s Point near Simon''s Town to Gordon''s Bay — covers approximately 28 km of open ocean. There is no protection from swell once away from the shore, and conditions deteriorate rapidly once the south-easterly wind builds, usually by mid-morning in summer.

The shark dimension is unique among the world''s major crossings. False Bay is one of the few places where great white sharks congregate in numbers, drawn by the Cape fur seal colony at Seal Island in the centre of the bay. Any crossing attempt requires dedicated shark watch boats and an experienced team familiar with local wildlife protocols.

The crossing has been completed by a handful of elite South African open water swimmers. It is not a commercially organised event — every attempt is a private expedition requiring significant planning, permits and a well-prepared support team.',
  preparation = '["Peak weekly volume: 40–55 km/week in the 12 weeks before your attempt","Longest training swim: 6–8 hours continuous in open water","Cold acclimatisation: regular swims in 14–16°C water — the Atlantic side of the Cape Peninsula is ideal","Ocean swimming experience: extensive experience with Cape conditions, swell and current","Night swimming optional but beneficial — start times before dawn","Cape Town''s Atlantic Seaboard and False Bay both offer excellent training grounds. DUC (Down Under Club) at Clifton provides cold Atlantic open water experience. False Bay spots like St James, Fish Hoek and Gordon''s Bay allow swimmers to familiarise themselves with the bay''s characteristics.","Planning a False Bay crossing requires: permits from the relevant marine authority, a dedicated support team with shark watch boats, a meteorological briefing from a local specialist, and an emergency plan. The crossing is not undertaken lightly and is generally only attempted by swimmers with extensive local open water credentials."]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"q":"Is it safe to swim in False Bay given the sharks?","a":"No crossing of False Bay can be considered without dedicated shark watch support. Multiple boats, shark spotters with experience in False Bay, and a clear abort protocol are required. Great white sightings mid-crossing are not uncommon, and any responsible attempt accounts for this with a robust safety plan."},{"q":"What is the best time of year to attempt False Bay?","a":"January to March offers the warmest water (16–18°C) and the most settled mornings. The window is narrow — the south-east wind activates reliably from late morning, so starts before 05:30 are typical to maximise calm water hours."},{"q":"How does False Bay compare to Robben Island?","a":"Robben Island (7.5 km) is the Cape Town crossing most swimmers target first. False Bay is nearly four times the distance, with the addition of shark risk and afternoon wind exposure. Completing Robben Island multiple times is considered a minimum prerequisite for planning False Bay."},{"q":"Who has swum False Bay?","a":"A small number of South African marathon swimmers have completed crossings of False Bay. It is not a regularly contested event — each crossing is a privately organised expedition. The crossing gained wider attention as South African open water swimming grew through the 2010s."}]'::jsonb,
  duration_text = NULL,
  governing_body = NULL,
  season_start_month = 1,
  season_end_month = 3,
  start_location = 'Miller''s Point (Simon''s Town)',
  end_location = 'Gordon''s Bay',
  oceans_seven = NULL,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE slug = 'false-bay';

UPDATE public.crossings SET
  hazards = '["Tidal currents of up to 5 knots run through the waters around the Channel Islands — among the strongest in the world. A swimmer caught in the wrong tidal phase is physically unable to make progress toward France regardless of fitness.","The Alderney Race — the turbulent passage between Alderney and Cap de la Hague — generates violent, confused seas and eddies that can make the final kilometres of the crossing extremely hazardous.","Water temperature of 14–18°C demands sustained cold tolerance over a 6–10 hour crossing. Core temperature management is critical, particularly in longer crossings.","Shipping traffic: the English Channel is one of the world''s busiest shipping lanes. Vessels transiting the Channel Separation Scheme operate in the waters between Jersey and France and must be navigated around."]'::jsonb,
  conditions = '["Neap tides (when the difference between high and low water is smallest) are essential for a crossing attempt — spring tides generate currents too powerful for most swimmers to make any northward progress.","The crossing must be timed to start on the north-going tide, ride it toward France, and land before the tide turns strongly southward again. The tidal window is typically 6–8 hours wide.","Fog is common in the English Channel in summer — navigation via GPS on the escort boat is essential, and fog horns may be needed to alert larger vessels.","Swell from Atlantic weather systems can combine with tidal currents to produce steep, confused seas that are physically exhausting and difficult to breathe in."]'::jsonb,
  about_md = 'Jersey is the largest of the Channel Islands — a British Crown dependency sitting closer to France than to England, in the Gulf of Saint-Malo. The island''s proximity to the Normandy coast and its extraordinary tidal environment have made the crossing to France a natural target for adventurous open water swimmers. The tidal range at Jersey regularly exceeds 10 metres during spring tides, and the currents this generates are among the most powerful of any inhabited coastline in the world.

The standard crossing route departs from St Ouen''s Bay on Jersey''s long, exposed west coast and aims for Cap de la Hague, the northwestern tip of the Cotentin Peninsula in Normandy. The peninsula''s headland creates significant tidal acceleration and the notorious Alderney Race to its north — a stretch of water that produces standing waves, eddies, and confused seas during strong tidal flows. Successful crossings typically aim to reach the French coast before engaging with the Race in its most active phase.

The crossing is far less institutionalised than the English Channel swim or the Oceans Seven crossings. There is no single governing body equivalent to the Channel Swimming Association, and sanctioned crossings are organised individually between swimmers, pilots, and local authorities. This means more flexibility for independent attempts but also less support infrastructure — the swimmer and their crew must manage more of the logistics themselves.

The Channel Islands'' relationship with the tide gives the Jersey-to-France crossing a unique character. Every decision — start time, route, pacing — is subordinate to the tide. The most experienced English Channel swimmers have been humbled by Channel Islands tides when crossing from Jersey; the currents here are stronger than anything encountered between Dover and Calais. Local pilot knowledge is not optional — it is the single most important factor in a successful crossing.',
  preparation = '["Longest single open water swim of at least 15–18km in cold water (below 17°C) before your attempt.","Weekly volume of 30–40km maintained through the summer, with at least one long cold water sea swim per week.","Cold acclimatisation: sustained swimming in 14–16°C water for 5+ hours without a wetsuit — this is the critical physical test for the crossing.","Tidal current swimming experience — practise in tidal estuaries or tidal pools to develop an intuitive sense of working with and against water movement.","English Channel qualifying swim is a useful benchmark — many Jersey-to-France aspirants complete an EC crossing first to establish their cold water and long-distance credentials.","Cold water tolerance is the most important physical preparation requirement for the Jersey to France crossing. At 14–16°C, the water is significantly colder than most open water swimming destinations, and maintaining efficient stroke mechanics over 6–10 hours in this temperature requires months of progressive cold water exposure. Start your cold acclimatisation in winter — swimming through the colder months rather than stopping and restarting in spring is far more effective at building genuine cold tolerance.","Pilot selection is the most consequential decision you will make for this crossing. Find a local Jersey or Normandy pilot with specific experience of this crossing or equivalent Channel Islands tidal waters. They need to understand the local tidal diamond data, the behaviour of currents around the Paternosters reef system north of Jersey, and the timing of the Alderney Race. A pilot who knows the English Channel well but has no Channel Islands experience is not sufficient — the tidal complexity here is of a different character to the Dover Strait."]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"q":"Why does the tidal range make such a big difference?","a":"When 12 metres of water moves in and out of a relatively enclosed bay over 6 hours, the resulting currents are enormous — think of it as a vast volume of water being forced through relatively narrow passages between islands and reefs. In the Channel Islands, spring tidal currents regularly exceed 5 knots (9km/h). No human swimmer can make forward progress against a 5-knot current. The entire crossing must be planned to work with the tide rather than against it, which constrains start time, route, and acceptable weather windows to a very narrow set of options each month."},{"q":"What is a neap tide and why is it important?","a":"Neap tides occur when the sun and moon are at right angles relative to Earth — their gravitational pulls partially cancel out, producing smaller tidal ranges and weaker currents. For Jersey, neap tides cut the tidal range from 12m (spring) to around 4–5m, and current speeds drop to a level where a strong swimmer can make useful progress across the tide. Spring tides are typically 7–14 days from neap tides. Your crossing window will be planned around the neap tide period each month between July and September."},{"q":"Is a wetsuit permitted?","a":"There is no formal governing body for the Jersey to France crossing with established rules equivalent to the Channel Swimming Association, so wetsuit or non-wetsuit is a personal choice that affects only your own record of the swim. If you are documenting the crossing for marathon swimming ratification purposes, English Channel rules (no wetsuit) would typically apply. Discuss the ratification standards you want to meet with your pilot and observer before the attempt."},{"q":"How close is Jersey to France exactly?","a":"The closest point between Jersey and the French coast is approximately 14km — from the northeast of the island to the Normandy coast near Granville. However, the most swum crossing is from St Ouen''s Bay on the west coast to Cap de la Hague, which is approximately 22km in a straight line. The Alderney Race and tidal considerations make the western route the more practical approach for most swimmers despite the longer straight-line distance."},{"q":"Do I need to cross an international border?","a":"Yes. Jersey is a British Crown dependency and France is an EU member state, so the crossing involves crossing an international maritime boundary. In practice, most crossings notify the relevant maritime authorities (Jersey Coastguard and French maritime authorities) in advance and carry the necessary documentation. Your pilot will be familiar with the notification requirements. Border formalities on arrival in France are handled at the nearest port — Cap de la Hague itself is not a port of entry, so your support vessel will typically proceed to Cherbourg or a nearby harbour for customs clearance."}]'::jsonb,
  duration_text = '6–10 hrs',
  governing_body = NULL,
  season_start_month = 7,
  season_end_month = 9,
  start_location = 'St Ouen''s Bay, Jersey (west coast)',
  end_location = 'Cap de la Hague, Normandy',
  oceans_seven = true,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE slug = 'jersey-to-france';

UPDATE public.crossings SET
  hazards = '["Tidal window is rigid: the swim must start at a precise time to ride the flood tide up the East River and ebb down the Hudson. Miss the window and you swim against current — virtually impossible to finish.","Heavy commercial vessel traffic — ferries, barges, and tugs — operates continuously. Your escort boat must actively shield you from wash and maintain communication with port control.","Water quality varies significantly by section — the Harlem River historically has the poorest water quality of the three waterways, with elevated bacterial counts after rain.","Debris in the water: logs, plastic, and urban detritus are common, particularly after rain events. Eye and mouth protection matters."]'::jsonb,
  conditions = '["Currents run up to 4–5 knots in the East River narrows — with the tide this is an enormous assist; against it, progress is nearly impossible.","The Hell Gate section (junction of East River, Harlem River, and Long Island Sound) produces complex, turbulent water with boils and eddies that can spin swimmers off course.","Wind funnelling between Manhattan''s skyscrapers can create localised chop in the Hudson despite benign offshore conditions.","Water temperatures in the Hudson River can change rapidly with tidal exchange — be prepared for cold patches without warning."]'::jsonb,
  about_md = 'The Manhattan Island Marathon Swim has been conducted since 1982, when Byron Bright became the first to complete the circumnavigation in an officially sanctioned event. The swim follows the perimeter of Manhattan Island: starting at the Battery (the island''s southern tip), heading north up the Hudson River along the west side, turning east through the Spuyten Duyvil Creek into the Harlem River, continuing east and south through the East River, navigating the infamous Hell Gate, and finishing back at the Battery.

The entire challenge is built around tidal timing. The East River is not actually a river — it is a tidal strait connecting Upper New York Bay to Long Island Sound. The currents in the East River run north on the flood tide and south on the ebb. A swimmer must ride the flood north, reach Hell Gate before the tide turns, and then ride the ebb back south. Miss any of these transitions and the swim becomes physically impossible without extraordinary effort. MIMS calculates the precise start time for each attempt months in advance based on tidal predictions.

The swim is the third leg of the Triple Crown of Open Water Swimming, alongside the English Channel and the Catalina Channel. Completing all three earns a swimmer one of open water''s most prestigious recognitions. As of 2024, fewer than 250 individuals have achieved the Triple Crown, reflecting the logistics, expense, and training required to complete three major crossings across different oceans.

The water quality of New York''s rivers has improved dramatically since the 1970s but remains variable. The Clean Water Act''s impact on the Hudson is remarkable — the river now supports fish and wildlife that had been absent for generations. However, combined sewer overflow events after heavy rain still degrade water quality temporarily, and MIMS monitors conditions closely before each sanctioned swim.',
  preparation = '["Longest single open water swim of at least 30km — the sheer distance of MIMS requires proven ultra-distance capacity.","Weekly training volume of 50–60km in the 3 months prior to your attempt.","River and current swimming experience — practise in tidal or current-affected water to understand drift management and pacing adjustments.","Night swimming experience — many MIMS starts are in early morning darkness for tidal window reasons.","Feed efficiency: eating and drinking must not break your stroke rhythm. Practise continuous swimming with feed stops under 15 seconds.","The Manhattan Island circumnavigation is unusual among marathon swims in that speed matters more than in channel crossings — not because of distance, but because of the tidal window. Swimmers who fall behind pace risk missing the current transition at Hell Gate, which effectively ends the attempt. Your preparation should include tempo sets and race-pace training at realistic speeds, not just slow ultra-distance endurance work. Know your pace per kilometre in open water with high confidence.","Logistics preparation is as demanding as physical preparation. MIMS requires coordination with the US Coast Guard, New York City Department of Parks and Recreation, and port authorities. You need a MIMS-approved observer, a suitable escort vessel, and a pilot familiar with New York Harbor tidal patterns. Most swimmers apply to MIMS a year in advance for an official event slot, rather than attempting an independent sanctioned swim. The official annual MIMS event in June handles much of the permitting complexity on behalf of entrants."]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"q":"Why does the swim go counterclockwise around Manhattan?","a":"The counterclockwise direction (down the Hudson, up the East River) is dictated by tidal timing. Swimmers ride the ebb tide south down the Hudson to the Battery, then the flood tide north up the East River and through Hell Gate. The tides in the two waterways operate on slightly offset schedules, making this the only direction that allows a swimmer to have current assistance for the majority of the course."},{"q":"Is the water safe to swim in?","a":"Generally yes, under normal conditions. MIMS monitors water quality and will postpone or cancel an attempt if bacterial levels are unsafe — typically after heavy rainfall that triggers sewer overflow. Swimmers are advised to keep their mouths closed when possible in the Harlem River section and to shower immediately after finishing. The rivers are dramatically cleaner than they were 40 years ago."},{"q":"Are wetsuits permitted?","a":"For a Triple Crown-ratified MIMS completion, English Channel rules apply: no wetsuit. The official MIMS event has historically been a non-wetsuit event. Independent sanctioned attempts seeking Triple Crown ratification must also be wetsuit-free. Check current MIMS rules as policies have evolved over time."},{"q":"How do I enter the official MIMS event?","a":"Apply through the MIMS website (swimaroundny.com). The annual event has limited entry and requires a demonstrated marathon swim history. Applications open in the preceding autumn. You can also apply for an independently sanctioned attempt outside the annual event, which requires more direct permitting work but offers more flexibility on date."},{"q":"What happens at Hell Gate?","a":"Hell Gate is the name for the turbulent confluence of the East River, Harlem River, and Long Island Sound near Astoria, Queens. The complex geometry of the waterways creates powerful eddies, standing waves, and boils when the tide is running strongly. Swimmers typically experience the most disorienting and physically demanding water of the entire circumnavigation in this section — usually around hours 4–6 of the swim."}]'::jsonb,
  duration_text = '7–9 hrs',
  governing_body = 'MIMS',
  season_start_month = 6,
  season_end_month = 9,
  start_location = NULL,
  end_location = NULL,
  oceans_seven = NULL,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE slug = 'manhattan-island';

UPDATE public.crossings SET
  hazards = '["Box jellyfish (Alatina alata) bloom predictably 9–10 nights after each full moon as they migrate from deep water to shallow reef areas — crossing dates must account for this cycle.","Tiger sharks are resident in Hawaiian waters year-round and are considered responsible for the majority of shark incidents in Hawaii. The channel''s deep water is prime hunting territory.","Pacific trade wind swells of 1.5–3m hit swimmers from the northeast for the majority of the crossing, creating relentless energy expenditure and stroke disruption over 15+ hours.","The channel reaches depths of over 800m — the psychological reality of swimming over genuinely deep, dark open ocean for a full day is a significant mental challenge."]'::jsonb,
  conditions = '["The Ka''iwi Channel is consistently rough — even in summer, the northeast trade winds generate a reliable 1–2m swell that rarely abates.","Strong surface currents can push swimmers significantly south of the target finish at Sandy Beach, adding kilometres to the actual distance covered.","Swims typically start at night from Molokai to take advantage of calmer pre-dawn conditions and arrive at Oahu in daylight.","Seasickness is a significant issue for support crew on escort boats in this channel — experienced ocean crews are essential."]'::jsonb,
  about_md = 'The Ka''iwi Channel — translated from Hawaiian as "Channel of Bones," named for the many sailors and fishermen who perished in its waters — is the stretch of Pacific Ocean between the islands of Molokai and Oahu. At approximately 42km, it is one of the longer Oceans Seven crossings, and the powerful trade wind swells that characterise Hawaiian waters mean swimmers face sustained rough-water conditions from start to finish. The channel has no shelter, no landmarks to aim at for most of the crossing, and depths that exceed 800 metres.

The first swim of the Ka''iwi Channel is attributed to Keo Nakama in 1961. The crossing gained international attention when Lynne Cox swam it in 1978 in 11 hours 32 minutes, a record that stood for many years. Today, the Molokai Channel is one of the most sought-after Oceans Seven crossings for international marathon swimmers, both because of its genuine difficulty and because Hawaii is a compelling destination for crew and support teams.

The box jellyfish planning constraint is unique among major crossings. Alatina alata, the box jellyfish species found in Hawaiian waters, migrates from deep water to reef areas in predictable blooms occurring roughly 9–10 days after each full moon. During these blooms, which typically last 2–3 nights, jellyfish numbers in the channel can be extraordinary — stings during a bloom can incapacitate a swimmer within minutes. All serious Ka''iwi Channel attempts are planned around the lunar calendar to avoid these windows, significantly constraining the scheduling of any given attempt.

Tiger sharks in Hawaiian waters are a genuine risk. Hawaii has the highest rate of unprovoked tiger shark incidents of any US state, and the deep, prey-rich waters of the Ka''iwi Channel are known shark habitat. Support boats maintain constant watch, and most experienced Hawaiian channel swimmers accept the risk as part of the challenge. Shark deterrent devices (electronic or magnetic) are sometimes used but are not universally effective against tiger sharks.',
  preparation = '["Longest single open water swim of at least 30km — the Ka''iwi Channel demands proven ultra-distance capacity in rough water.","Weekly training volume of 50–60km maintained for at least 3–4 months prior to the attempt.","Sustained rough water swimming — you must be able to maintain efficient stroke mechanics in 1.5–2m swell for 15+ hours.","Night swimming experience: most attempts start between midnight and 3am and run through the following day.","Jellyfish sting response: know your personal reaction to jellyfish stings and have a treatment protocol agreed with your crew and medical advisor.","Unlike most Oceans Seven crossings, the Molokai Channel does not require cold water acclimatisation — at 24–28°C the water is comfortably warm throughout the season. The preparation focus instead shifts entirely to ultra-distance endurance in rough ocean conditions. Swimmers who train primarily in pools or protected harbours often find the relentless Pacific swell far more energy-consuming than their training suggested. Build in as much open ocean swell swimming as possible in the months before your attempt, even if this means travelling to exposed coastlines for training camps.","Nutrition over 15–20 hours is a significant logistical challenge. Most Ka''iwi Channel swimmers consume 600–800 calories per hour in liquid form, primarily carbohydrate-based drinks with sodium supplementation. Crew briefing is critical — they need to maintain your feeding schedule precisely, monitor your stroke count and pace, and manage the boat safely in open Pacific conditions. Engage an experienced Hawaiian crew well in advance; local knowledge of the channel''s currents is an enormous advantage for your pilot in choosing the correct start angle and adjusting for drift during the swim."]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"q":"How do I plan around the box jellyfish cycle?","a":"Box jellyfish in Hawaii bloom approximately 9–10 nights after each full moon, with the peak typically on night 9–10 and tapering off over 2–3 nights. Identify the full moon dates during your available window, count forward 9–10 days, and avoid those nights. Your target swim window should be at least 4–5 days before or after a predicted bloom. Local marine biologists and experienced Hawaii channel crews can advise on current seasonal patterns."},{"q":"Is the Molokai Channel governed by a specific organisation?","a":"Unlike some Oceans Seven crossings, the Ka''iwi Channel does not have a single dedicated governing body equivalent to the CCSF or CSSA. Ratification for Oceans Seven purposes is handled through the Oceans Seven governing framework. Swimmers organise their own sanctioned attempts with locally approved observers and pilots. The Hawaii Swimming Association and experienced local operators can advise on the requirements for a ratified crossing."},{"q":"What is the shark situation — should I be worried?","a":"Tiger sharks are a genuine risk that all Ka''iwi Channel swimmers accept. The channel''s deep water and proximity to reef systems that support prey species make it active shark habitat. Your escort boat provides the primary deterrent — sharks typically avoid large vessels. Some swimmers use electronic shark deterrent devices. There have been no fatal attacks during sanctioned Ka''iwi Channel attempts, but sharks are seen regularly by support crews."},{"q":"Can I swim it in winter?","a":"Winter swims (October–March) are possible but much more challenging. North Pacific storms generate large northwest swells that stack on top of the resident trade-wind swell, producing chaotic 3–5m seas. The few winter crossings on record are exceptional feats of rough-water swimming. The April–September window offers significantly more manageable conditions for a first crossing."},{"q":"Where do I finish on Oahu?","a":"The traditional finish is Sandy Beach on Oahu''s southeast coast (also known as Makapuu area). Depending on currents during the swim, some crossings finish slightly north or south of Sandy Beach — your pilot will adjust the target finish based on actual drift experienced during the crossing. A shore touch is required for ratification."}]'::jsonb,
  duration_text = '14–18 hrs',
  governing_body = NULL,
  season_start_month = 4,
  season_end_month = 9,
  start_location = 'Laau Point, Molokai',
  end_location = 'Sandy Beach, Oahu',
  oceans_seven = true,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE slug = 'molokai-channel';

UPDATE public.crossings SET
  hazards = '["Water temperature as low as 10–12°C — sustained hypothermia risk through any attempt","Lion''s mane jellyfish (Cyanea capillata) — the largest jellyfish in the world, peak season July–August","Powerful tidal streams that can push swimmers miles off course if mistimed","Shipping traffic in the North Channel maritime corridor"]'::jsonb,
  conditions = '["Neap tides are preferred — spring tides create current that makes the crossing nearly impossible","Wind windows are narrow; the Irish Sea is notorious for rapid weather changes","Sea state can deteriorate quickly — swells of 1–2m are common during attempts","Thick jellyfish blooms in some years mean contact is unavoidable at night"]'::jsonb,
  about_md = 'The North Channel separates Northern Ireland from southwest Scotland. The shortest viable route runs from Donaghadee Harbour on the Ards Peninsula to Portpatrick on the Rhins of Galloway — roughly 35 km as the crow flies, considerably more through the water.

The first ratified crossing was completed by Tom Blower in 1947, after earlier attempts dating back to the late 19th century. For decades it remained one of the rarest accomplishments in open water swimming. It was designated one of the Oceans Seven by Steven Munatones in 2008, alongside the English Channel, Catalina, Cook Strait, Molokai Channel, Tsugaru Strait and Strait of Gibraltar.

The jellyfish problem is unlike anywhere else in the Oceans Seven. Lion''s mane tentacles can trail 3–4 metres and deliver intense stings. Swimmers have reported full-body welts requiring post-swim hospitalisation. Many North Channel veterans describe the jellyfish as psychologically as hard as the cold.

Cold water acclimatisation is non-negotiable. Most successful swimmers spend 12–18 months swimming through Irish or Scottish winters, building tolerance below 10°C. No amount of English Channel preparation fully substitutes for North Channel cold.',
  preparation = '["Peak weekly volume: 50–65 km/week in the 12 weeks before your attempt","Longest training swim: 8–10 hours continuous (not just distance — time matters)","Cold acclimatisation: regular swims at or below 12°C for 6+ months","Night swimming: at least 2–3 night ocean sessions in the months before (jellyfish are worse at night)","Feeding practice: comfortable taking feeds at 45-minute intervals without stopping","Most North Channel swimmers train in Ireland or the west coast of Scotland from October through the winter to build cold tolerance in conditions that replicate what they''ll face on crossing day. Dover Harbour is useful for English Channel prep; the North Channel requires Irish Sea cold.","Booking through the ICA typically requires completing a qualifying swim of at least 8 hours in cold water. Many pilots require evidence of cold water tolerance before they will accept a booking. Budget 2–3 years of preparation for most swimmers attempting this crossing."]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"q":"Why is the North Channel harder than the English Channel?","a":"The English Channel is 2–4°C warmer and has virtually no lion''s mane jellyfish. The North Channel''s combination of cold water, powerful tides and jellyfish makes it significantly harder — most open water experts rate it the hardest of the Oceans Seven."},{"q":"How do you deal with lion''s mane jellyfish?","a":"You don''t avoid them — you endure them. Some swimmers use a thin layer of lanolin for minor protection. Most accept stings as part of the crossing. Antihistamines are carried on escort boats but rarely eliminate the pain. Mental preparation for jellyfish contact is considered as important as physical training."},{"q":"How cold does the water get?","a":"The warmest recorded crossing-day temperatures are around 14°C in late August. July attempts often see 10–12°C. In cold years, even September water can be 11°C. The crossing requires sustained swimming through temperatures that would cause hypothermia within 1–2 hours for an untrained swimmer."},{"q":"What is the Oceans Seven?","a":"The Oceans Seven is marathon swimming''s equivalent of the Seven Summits — seven iconic channel crossings worldwide: English Channel, North Channel, Catalina Channel, Cook Strait, Molokai Channel, Tsugaru Strait and Strait of Gibraltar. The North Channel is widely regarded as the hardest of the seven."},{"q":"How do I book a North Channel attempt?","a":"Contact the Infinity Channel Association (ICA) for ratification requirements. Pilots operate out of Donaghadee. Bookings are taken 12–18 months in advance for peak season windows. You will need evidence of a qualifying swim and cold-water experience before a pilot will accept your booking."}]'::jsonb,
  duration_text = NULL,
  governing_body = NULL,
  season_start_month = 7,
  season_end_month = 9,
  start_location = NULL,
  end_location = NULL,
  oceans_seven = true,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE slug = 'north-channel';

UPDATE public.crossings SET
  hazards = '["The channel is open ocean — swells of 1–3m from the southwest are common in February, creating relentless side chop that increases energy expenditure significantly.","Blue-ringed octopus and stinging jellyfish (particularly Chrysaora species) are present in these waters, though encounters during the event are rare.","Current variability: the Leeuwin Current can push swimmers off course and create fatigue-inducing drift that adds distance if navigation is poor.","Heat and sun exposure over a multi-hour swim in February''s summer sun can cause dehydration — liquid nutrition management is critical."]'::jsonb,
  conditions = '["Swell direction is typically from the southwest, hitting swimmers on the right side for most of the crossing heading westward.","Wind chop overlays the swell in the afternoon; most elite swimmers aim to finish before conditions deteriorate after midday.","Water clarity is exceptional — visibility often exceeds 10m — which can be psychologically challenging over deep, open ocean.","The final kilometres approaching Thomson Bay often see a current pushing swimmers north; pilots guide boat-swimmer pairs to compensate."]'::jsonb,
  about_md = 'The Rottnest Channel Swim began in 1990 with a handful of swimmers and has grown into one of the iconic events on the global open water calendar. The channel between the Western Australian mainland and Rottnest Island — known to the Noongar people as Wadjemup — covers 19.7km of exposed Indian Ocean. The crossing departs from Cottesloe Beach, one of Perth''s most beloved swimming beaches, and finishes at Thomson Bay on the island''s eastern shore.

What makes the Rottnest Swim unique in world open water swimming is its scale: over 2,500 swimmers enter across solo, duo, and team-of-four categories, supported by hundreds of escort vessels and thousands of volunteers. The event is so popular that entries typically sell out within minutes of opening, often months in advance. The presence of so many swimmers on the water simultaneously creates a festive, community atmosphere very different from solitary marathon crossings.

Solo swimming is the most demanding category and draws an international field of elite marathon swimmers each year. The current solo record is held by Tommy Mayne (2019) at 3 hours 56 minutes. The duo category — where two swimmers alternate in 90-minute legs — is the most popular entry point for recreational swimmers tackling the channel for the first time.

Every swimmer must have a registered escort vessel and a paddler or kayaker alongside them in the water. Pilots are experienced local operators who know the current patterns of the channel intimately and guide swimmers on the most efficient line to Thomson Bay.',
  preparation = '["Longest single open water swim of at least 12–15km before race day.","Consistent weekly volume of 25–35km in the 8 weeks prior to the event.","Swell training: practise in ocean swells to adapt your stroke rhythm and breathing to a moving waterline.","Feed practice: can you eat and drink from a kayak or boat while treading water or swimming slowly? Practise this in open water.","For duo and team entries, coordination and transition drills between swimmers in open water are important.","Unlike many marathon crossings, the Rottnest Swim takes place in relatively warm water (18–22°C), which removes the cold water acclimatisation demand that dominates preparation for northern hemisphere crossings. The primary training focus for solos is distance endurance and swell tolerance. If you''re training in protected pool or harbour water, build in at least one long ocean swim per week through your preparation block to adapt to the physical and psychological demands of open water movement.","Nutrition planning is as important as physical preparation for solos. A 5–7 hour swim in summer heat requires disciplined hydration and carbohydrate intake. Most experienced Rottnest soloists take a liquid feed (electrolyte drink with maltodextrin) every 20–30 minutes. Brief the paddler supporting you in water on your feeding schedule and what to watch for — signs of heat stress, salt intake, and stroke count degradation are the key indicators your paddler should monitor."]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"q":"Can I wear a wetsuit?","a":"Yes. Unlike English Channel-style crossings, wetsuits are permitted in all categories of the Rottnest Channel Swim. However, solo category results are split into wetsuit and non-wetsuit divisions, and the overall solo winners are typically non-wetsuit swimmers. Many recreational participants choose wetsuits for buoyancy and comfort over the longer distance."},{"q":"How do entries work — are they hard to get?","a":"Extremely. Entries open once per year (typically July–August for the following February event) and sell out within hours, sometimes minutes. Solo entries are particularly limited. Register for notifications on the official Rottnest Channel Swim website and be ready to enter the moment the ballot or direct sale opens. Many swimmers enter the ballot multiple years in a row."},{"q":"What is a pilot and do I need one?","a":"A registered pilot is a licensed boat operator who escorts your swimmer or team across the channel. Every entry must have a registered pilot vessel — this is mandatory and cannot be substituted by a friend with a tinnie. Pilots are booked through the official pilot registration system and must meet event safety requirements. Most swimmers book pilots as soon as their entry is confirmed."},{"q":"Is there a minimum qualifier required?","a":"Solo swimmers must demonstrate a qualifying swim: typically a sanctioned open water swim of at least 10km completed within a specified timeframe. Duo and team-of-four entrants have lower qualifying requirements. Full qualifying standards are published on the official event website and updated annually."},{"q":"What happens if weather is too rough on race day?","a":"The event has a dedicated safety and weather committee. If conditions are deemed unsafe (typically swell height exceeding safe thresholds), the event is postponed to the following day or, in rare cases, cancelled. This has occurred a handful of times in the event''s history. Swimmers should plan accommodation and travel with the possibility of a one-day postponement in mind."}]'::jsonb,
  duration_text = '4–7 hrs (solo)',
  governing_body = NULL,
  season_start_month = 2,
  season_end_month = 2,
  start_location = 'Cottesloe Beach',
  end_location = 'Thomson Bay, Rottnest Island',
  oceans_seven = NULL,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE slug = 'rottnest-channel';

UPDATE public.crossings SET
  hazards = '["Cold upwelling: the Tsugaru Current carries warm Kuroshio water northeast, but deep cold water upwells without warning — surface temperature can drop from 20°C to 10°C within metres, causing sudden cold shock in a swimmer who felt comfortable moments before.","Current variability: the strait lies between two major current systems (Tsugaru Current and Liman Cold Current) whose interaction creates unpredictable, shifting current patterns that no tidal table can fully capture.","Extremely narrow weather window: typhoons regularly affect Japan''s northern waters from July through September, and the Tsugaru Strait is frequently closed to small vessels by Japan Coast Guard during typhoon watches. A week''s window may realistically offer only 1–2 viable swim days.","Logistical complexity: all correspondence with TCSA and authorities is in Japanese; obtaining vessel permits, Coast Guard notifications, and observer assignments requires extensive advance planning, often with a Japanese-speaking intermediary."]'::jsonb,
  conditions = '["The strait sees strong winds from both north and south depending on synoptic patterns — calm days with manageable swell are relatively rare in the August–September window.","Fog is extremely common in the Tsugaru Strait, particularly in early morning — the Japan Coast Guard may ground escort boats in zero-visibility fog, aborting a swim already underway.","Ferry traffic between Honshu and Hokkaido (particularly the Tsugaru Kaikyo Ferry route) creates hazardous vessel wash and requires active management by the pilot vessel.","The strait marks the boundary between the Sea of Japan and the Pacific Ocean, where two distinct water masses meet — the oceanographic complexity this creates drives the unpredictable conditions swimmers face."]'::jsonb,
  about_md = 'The Tsugaru Strait (津軽海峡, Tsugaru Kaikyo) separates the Japanese islands of Honshu and Hokkaido at Japan''s northeastern extremity. At its narrowest point the strait is approximately 19.5km wide, connecting the Sea of Japan to the west with the Pacific Ocean (specifically the Tsugaru Strait section of the western North Pacific) to the east. The crossing runs from the cape of Tappi Saki on the Aomori Prefecture coast of Honshu to Shirakami Misaki on the southern tip of Hokkaido.

The Tsugaru Strait is one of the most important strategic waterways in East Asia — it is one of the few deep-water passages through which large vessels (including submarines) can transit between the Pacific and the Sea of Japan. This military and commercial significance means the Japan Coast Guard maintains active oversight of all vessel movements in the strait, adding a layer of regulatory complexity to any crossing attempt that does not exist for most other marathon swims in the world.

The Tsugaru Channel Swimming Association was established to support and ratify crossings of the strait and to manage the necessary government coordination. Because the TCSA operates entirely in Japanese and most of its processes are documented only in Japanese, international swimmers face a significant communication barrier. Unlike other Oceans Seven crossings where English-language information and local English-speaking crews are readily available, Tsugaru crossings typically require either fluency in Japanese or a trusted Japanese-speaking intermediary to coordinate the logistics. This practical difficulty is itself part of what makes the crossing rare and prestigious.

The first recorded crossing is attributed to a Japanese swimmer in 1955. The crossing remains one of the least-attempted of the Oceans Seven, with only a handful of successful international completions each year — far fewer than Gibraltar or the Catalina Channel. The combination of logistical difficulty, narrow weather window, and unpredictable cold upwelling means that many Oceans Seven chasers who successfully complete the other six spend years waiting for a Tsugaru attempt to align.',
  preparation = '["Sustained swimming in cold water, including experience with sudden temperature drops — acclimatise to entering 10–12°C water after extended swimming in 18–20°C water.","Longest single open water swim of at least 15km in exposed ocean conditions.","Weekly volume of 35–45km maintained through the spring and summer before your attempt.","Typhoon contingency planning: physical readiness to attempt on any viable day within your window means being match-fit for the entire 2–3 week stay in Japan.","Begin TCSA contact and logistics at least 12–18 months before your target season — the paperwork and permits required are substantial.","The cold upwelling hazard unique to the Tsugaru Strait deserves specific preparation attention. Unlike Cook Strait where the water is consistently cold throughout, Tsugaru can feel like warm water swimming for hours before suddenly delivering a pocket of 10°C deep water. The physiological shock of this transition — when your body has been warm and relaxed — can be more dangerous than sustained cold water swimming. Train specifically for this by practising deliberate transitions from warm to cold water during long sessions, building your nervous system''s ability to adapt quickly without panicking or losing stroke efficiency.","Logistics preparation should begin 12–18 months before your target crossing season. Contact the TCSA (ideally through a Japanese-speaking channel) to understand the current requirements for sanctioned attempts. Engage an experienced Japanese escort boat captain — the pool of operators willing and permitted to escort marathon swimmers in this heavily regulated strait is small. Many international swimmers work through a Japan-based open water swimming contact or sports tourism operator who can navigate the bureaucratic requirements. Budget substantially more time and money than for any other Oceans Seven crossing for the logistics phase alone."]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"q":"Why is the Tsugaru Strait considered so logistically difficult?","a":"Three factors combine to make Tsugaru the most logistically challenging Oceans Seven crossing. First, the Japan Coast Guard oversight of the strait means formal vessel permits and notifications are required — not just courtesy calls. Second, the TCSA operates entirely in Japanese with limited English-language support for international swimmers. Third, the narrow weather window (effectively a few weeks in August–September) concentrates demand and leaves almost no flexibility if paperwork isn''t completed months in advance. The swim itself is not the hardest part of a Tsugaru attempt — getting to the start line is."},{"q":"What is the cold upwelling and how do I prepare for it?","a":"The Tsugaru Strait sits at the boundary between the warm Tsugaru Current (carrying Kuroshio water northeast) and cold deep Pacific water. The interface between these water masses is not stable — cold, dense water periodically rises to the surface (upwells) in localised patches. A swimmer can be at 19°C surface water and suddenly enter a patch of 10–11°C upwelled deep water. The transition is abrupt and the patch may be 50–200m wide. Prepare by deliberately practising cold water entry after warm swimming, and mentally rehearsing your response to sudden cold shock: maintain stroke, breathe steadily, do not panic."},{"q":"How do I contact the Tsugaru Channel Swimming Association?","a":"The TCSA is based in Aomori Prefecture and primarily communicates in Japanese. The most reliable approach for international swimmers is to connect via the global Oceans Seven community — other swimmers who have completed Tsugaru can often provide current contact details and introductions. Some Japanese sports tourism operators specialise in facilitating international marathon swim attempts and can manage TCSA liaison on your behalf. Allow at least 12–18 months of lead time for the full sanctioning and permit process."},{"q":"What direction is the swim?","a":"The standard ratified direction is Honshu (Tappi Saki, Aomori Prefecture) to Hokkaido (Shirakami Misaki). This direction is preferred because the Tsugaru Current generally flows northeast through the strait, providing a mild assist and a more predictable drift pattern. The reverse crossing is technically possible but significantly harder and rarely attempted. Both directions are accepted for Oceans Seven ratification purposes."},{"q":"How does Tsugaru compare to the other Oceans Seven crossings?","a":"In terms of raw distance (19.5km), Tsugaru is one of the shorter Oceans Seven crossings — comparable to the Rottnest Channel Swim in straight-line distance. But it is consistently ranked among the most difficult Oceans Seven crossings due to the combination of cold upwelling, current variability, narrow weather window, logistical complexity, and the psychological challenge of an attempt that may be weather-aborted after years of preparation. Many Oceans Seven completists describe Tsugaru as the crossing that took the most attempts and the most total elapsed time between first application and successful completion."}]'::jsonb,
  duration_text = '7–12 hrs',
  governing_body = 'TCSA',
  season_start_month = 8,
  season_end_month = 9,
  start_location = 'Tappi Saki, Honshu',
  end_location = 'Shirakami Misaki, Hokkaido',
  oceans_seven = true,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE slug = 'tsugaru-strait';
